course: dictionary
lesson: 10048
title: Terrifying: scary, and more
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Terrifying» means making you feel extremely frightened. Read the clues around it: in «Being winded feels terrifying», the feeling is fear, so the word is about being scared.
explain: Sometimes the word is not about fear at all. People say «a terrifying amount of homework» to mean a huge, worrying amount. Ask what word would fit: huge fits, so here it means very worrying or amazing in a scary way.
explain: Look at how strong the word is. «Terrifying» is stronger than «scary» or «frightening». A gorilla beating his chest «looks terrifying», but the sentence goes on to say he is mostly a gentle vegetarian, so the look is scary while the truth is not.
fact: Terrifying is an adjective: terrifying, more terrifying, most terrifying.
fact: «Terrifying» means making you feel extremely frightened.
fact: Informal use: «a terrifying amount of homework» means very worrying or amazing in a scary way.
fact: Terrifying is stronger than scary or frightening.
fact: Terrifying is also the present participle of the verb terrify, which means to frighten greatly.
exercise: «The storm was terrifying, with lightning over the sea.» What does terrifying mean here? | Quiet and calm | Tasty | *Making you feel extremely frightened | Very tidy
exercise: Mum says, «We have a terrifying amount of washing up.» What does terrifying mean? | *Very worrying or amazing in a scary way | Hungry | Finished | Wet and cold
exercise: Which is stronger: scary or terrifying? | They are exactly the same strength | *Terrifying | Scary
test: «The lift got stuck, and the dark was terrifying.» What does terrifying suggest? | Full of light | Very quiet and calm | Very tasty | *Extremely frightening
test: Ella says the spelling test was terrifying. What is she probably saying? | *That it felt very worrying, like a scary challenge | That she won a prize | That it was a short test | That she finished it early and felt sleepy
exam: Which sentence uses terrifying in its informal sense? | «The lion roared and the crowd fled.» | *«The homework list was a terrifying length.» | «The lake was calm and blue.» | «Mum sang softly in the kitchen.»
